1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,160
He was Hitler's successor,

2
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:04,490
and eighteen years after the war he stood in front of a school class.

3
00:00:04,510 --> 00:00:06,370
His name was Karl Dönitz.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,910
As commander of the U-boats

5
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:10,750
he sent tens of thousands of young men into the Atlantic,

6
00:00:10,770 --> 00:00:12,580
and most of them never came back.

7
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,610
In his testament, Hitler named him Reich President.

8
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:18,870
From Flensburg-Mürwik, Dönitz governed for three more weeks

9
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:20,820
a Reich that barely existed anymore.

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:25,330
On 23 May 1945, British soldiers moved in.

11
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:28,160
Dönitz and his entire government were arrested.

12
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:30,960
At Nuremberg he was sentenced to ten years in prison,

13
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,730
which he served in Spandau to the very last day.

14
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:37,760
In January 1963 he came to a grammar school in Geesthacht.

15
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,530
The talk had been organised by the head boy, Uwe Barschel,

16
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:44,140
later Minister-President of Schleswig-Holstein.

17
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:46,980
Dönitz spoke to senior students about the war

18
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,540
without a word of criticism or remorse.

19
00:00:49,630 --> 00:00:51,310
Teachers and students applauded,

20
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:53,500
and the local paper praised the evening.

21
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,700
Then Der Spiegel, Die Zeit and even Le Monde reported on it,

22
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,810
and all of Germany was outraged.

23
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,450
The headmaster came under pressure

24
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:02,380
and took his own life shortly afterwards.

25
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:04,620
Dönitz himself died in 1980

26
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:07,150
and defended his role to the very end.
